{"schema":"plectis_maths_universe_excerpts_v2","paper":"erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface","excerpts":[["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:a9","Rationality gives an eventual tail\nperiod","<p><em> If <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi></msup><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> with\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>v</mi></math></span> a positive odd integer, then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.</mn></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Euler’s theorem gives <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>v</mi><mo>∣</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, and the prefix-tail identity\nthen makes <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>S</mi></math></span> integral.\nThus rationality supplies a positive shift for which all sufficiently\nlate tail differences are integral.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:d5","Rationality forces unbounded carry\nrank","<p><em> If <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></math></span>, there\nare an integer <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>v</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> and an\ninteger sequence <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi></math></span> such that </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟶</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> and, for every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>e</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, the sequences <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">↦</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi></msup><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>e</mi></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi></msup></math></span> span a space of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></math></span>-dimension at least <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>e</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>.</em></p>\n<p><em>The recurrence follows from the scaled tails after clearing a\nhypothetical denominator of <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi></math></span>. The\nonly growth condition imposed here is the displayed limit <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>N</mi></msup><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>; subexponential growth is\nnot assumed. The rank lower bound is a necessary consequence of\nrationality and yields no contradiction by itself. An upper bound using\nadditional properties of the actual totient coefficients would be\nneeded; the later rational comparison rules out such a bound for\narbitrary rational coefficient series.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:d4","Independence of the retained dyadic\nsections","<p><em> For every integer <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>e</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, the family </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi></msup><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>r</mi><mtext> odd</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> is linearly independent over <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></math></span> and has <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>e</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> members. At <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> this family still contains <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>; it is not the full\nlevel-zero truncation, which consists only of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. For <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>e</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> it is the basis of the full\ntruncated dyadic family.</em></p>\n<p><em>The proof separates one section at a time: the Chinese remainder\ntheorem forces suitable prime divisors in the other affine arguments,\nwhile Dirichlet’s theorem makes the selected argument prime. This proves\nindependence, not just the displayed cardinality. In particular, the\nspan of all dyadic sections is infinite-dimensional; that conclusion\nalone says nothing about rationality of their weighted sum.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:a8","Denominator divisibility forces tail\nintegrality","<p><em> If <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>q</mi></math></span> in\nlowest terms with <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo>∣</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span>. Indeed, the\nprefix identity expresses that difference as <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>S</mi></math></span> minus an integer. This is the\ncontradiction used by a nonintegrality certificate to exclude the\nrational value <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>q</mi></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:b5","A cyclotomic factor remains after\ncancellation","<p><em> If <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi></math></span> is\nsquarefree, then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>∣</mo><mi>den</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Here <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></math></span> is the <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi></math></span>-th cyclotomic polynomial.\nProposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#catalogue:mob:b4\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:b4\">6.40</a>\ngives <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.444em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>,\nand <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> because <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi></math></span> is squarefree. This proves that the\nindicated cyclotomic factor cannot cancel from <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:b6","Primes in the upper half remain\nafter cancellation","<p><em> For <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></math></span>\nthe whole product </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><munder><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mrow><mi>𝒫</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mo>∣</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>den</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> remains after\ncancellation. More generally, the source gives the same\nproduct-divisibility conclusion for a multiplier coprime to that\nproduct; it also supplies a sufficient condition on the multiplier’s\nprime factors. For the multiplier <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></math></span>, those prime factors are at most\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi></math></span>, and the LCM result above\nfollows.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:b7a","A lower bound for the reduced\ndenominator","<p><em> For every integer <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌊</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌋</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><munder><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mrow><mi>𝒫</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>den</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Bertrand’s postulate\nsupplies a prime in <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mrow><mi>𝒫</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></math></span>;\nits factor is at least <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌊</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌋</mo></mrow></msup></math></span>. Proposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#catalogue:mob:b6\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:b6\">6.6</a> then gives the\nsecond inequality. The complementary term in the real tail decomposition\ncan still cancel this rational contribution, so denominator growth alone\ndoes not prove irrationality of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:b7b","The exact reduced denominator","<p><em>\nAt every integer scale <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>den</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"></mspace><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mfrac><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><munder><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mtable columnspacing=\"0em\" rowspacing=\"0.1em\"><mtr><mtd><mi>p</mi><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>p</mi><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi>odd</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>prime</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></munder><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The product is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span> when there is no odd prime divisor.\nThis gives the exact cancellation, including the multiplier <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></math></span>, rather than merely a lower bound\nfor the denominator. For example, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>60</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></math></span>, and the denominator is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>30</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:mob:d3","Nonvanishing of a signed dyadic\nsum","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>I</mi></math></span> be finite, let\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span>, and suppose that <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></math></span> is the unique index with maximal\nexponent <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></math></span>. If <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></math></span> is odd, then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></msup><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mfrac><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo>≡</mo><mn>1</mn><mspace width=\"1em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Indeed, every\nsummand other than <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></math></span> is even. The\nsum is therefore nonzero. In the determinant application, the\nrectangular Cauchy–Binet formula produces such finite signed sums from\ntruncated moments. The result tests a specified configuration; it does\nnot show that a suitable maximal-exponent term exists at arbitrarily\nlarge scales.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:a11","Small certificates","<p><em> For each\ninteger <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></math></span>, the finite test\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>𝒞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> holds. Each\ndiscrepancy uses two 16-term windows. Across all eight shifts, only the\n24 distinct totient values at <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>13</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>36</mn></math></span> are needed, since the windows overlap. The source\nverifies the eight integer residue inequalities by exact\ncomputation.</em></p>\n<p><em>Consequently, if <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></math></span> is a\nreduced fraction with <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> and\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>∣</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>12</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> for at least one\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></math></span>, then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></math></span>. This follows from\nPropositions <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#catalogue:cert:a6\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:a6\">6.18</a> and <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#catalogue:cert:a8\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:a8\">6.4</a>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:a12","A common certificate for sixteen\nshifts","<p><em> For each integer <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>16</mn></math></span>, one has <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>𝒞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. The common basepoint is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>14</mn></math></span> and the depth is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>9</mn></math></span>; these parameters have different roles.\nThe two windows for each shift use only the 25 distinct totient values\nat <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>15</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>39</mn></math></span> across the whole\nfamily. Thus <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></math></span> for every\nreduced fraction with <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> such\nthat <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>∣</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>14</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> for at\nleast one <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>16</mn></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:b11","Historical diagonal examples and the\ncomplete band through 82","<p><em> Let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>lcm</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> and\nlet </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">:⟺</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi>𝒞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The checked certificate\ntable proves <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> at 28 explicit\nindices, ending at <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></math></span>. For the\ninitial indices </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>13</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>17</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em>\nthe corresponding depths begin <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>21</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>22</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>23</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>26</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo></math></span>.\nEach entry is a finite kernel computation on explicit totient values.\nThe factorisations use checked prime-power blocks and Lucas primality\ncertificates. Thus the table proves a finite list of instances of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>; it does not prove that <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> holds for infinitely many <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi></math></span>.</em></p>\n<p><em>The historical 28 examples are now a strict subset of the\naggregate theorem <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>82</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. That theorem closes the finite interval without holes but\nsupplies neither <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>83</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> nor a\ncofinal family.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:a2","The prefix-tail identity","<p><em> For\nevery <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>N</mi></msup><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></munder><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Thus\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>N</mi></msup><mi>S</mi></math></span> have the same fractional part.\nSubtracting the identities at <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></math></span>\nand <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>N</mi></math></span> gives the tail-difference\ncongruence used in the irrationality criterion.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:a5","The necessary depth inequality","<p><em> For\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span>, the condition\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>𝒞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> implies </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Indeed, its lower\nresidue bound must be smaller than its upper bound. Thus <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> whenever a\ncertificate holds. In particular, a fixed depth cannot accommodate\nunbounded <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></math></span>. This is a necessary\ndepth bound, not an upper bound for finding a certificate.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:a6","A certificate implies\nnonintegrality","<p><em> For all <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>𝒞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟹</mo><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The scaled truncation error\nsatisfies <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>. If the tail difference were integral, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> would therefore lie within that\ndistance of a multiple of <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup></math></span>,\ncontrary to the two strict residue inequalities.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:a7","Nonintegrality gives a certificate at\nsome depth","<p><em> For every <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi>𝒞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟺</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> For\nthe converse to Proposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#catalogue:cert:a6\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:a6\">6.18</a>, fix a positive\ndistance from the nonintegral tail difference to the nearest integer.\nThe normalised error <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup></math></span>\ntends to zero, so a sufficiently large truncation preserves that\ndistance. This is a pointwise equivalence; irrationality requires the\nstated quantifiers over the shift and basepoint.</em></p>"]],"source":"erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.json?v=4457e47d5986"}
